----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C -- which model the flow actually allows
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  framework WITHOUT the external field: effective braking mass at 1 Mpc = 1.23e+13 Msun, R0 = 2.72 Mpc -- 3.1 times the observed zero-velocity radius.  This is the MOND Local Group timing problem, and it is real
  framework WITH it: 1.74e+12 Msun, R0 = 1.09 Mpc -- the external field cuts the braking mass by a factor 7 and brings R0 to within 24 per cent of the measurement
  LambdaCDM with the independently measured halos: 2.80e+12 Msun, R0 = 1.28 Mpc
  Newtonian gravity with the baryons alone: 2.20e+11 Msun, R0 = 0.55 Mpc -- far too little braking, which is why the Local Group needs either dark matter or a boost
  [PASS] 99c (THE RESULT) the external field is what saves the framework here, and it was not free: with e_N taken unchanged from an entirely separate calculation on an entirely separate dataset, the Local Group's effective braking mass falls by an order of magnitude and the predicted zero-velocity radius lands near the measured one, where the same calculation with the external field ignored over-predicts it by a large factor   (observed R0 = 0.88 Mpc; framework + EFE 1.09; framework without EFE 2.72; LambdaCDM 1.28; Newtonian baryons 0.55)
  AND THE APPARENT PREFERENCE EVAPORATES ON A NUISANCE PARAMETER.  The framework's curve does fit better than LambdaCDM's at M = 2.8e+12 (36.0 against 36.5 km/s) -- but the Local Group's halo mass is uncertain by a factor of about two, and at the timing-argument value 1.9e12 LambdaCDM gives R0 = 1.12 Mpc and rms 37.0 km/s.  That must not be quoted as a preference for the framework.
  [PASS] 99d BUT THE FLOW DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE, and saying so is the honest half: the framework's and LambdaCDM's curves fit the 1-3 Mpc galaxies about equally well once LambdaCDM's halo mass is allowed its own factor-of-two uncertainty, because both are matched by nature to the same zero-velocity radius and the data's scatter is far larger than the difference between them   (rms about the framework's curve 36.0 km/s, about LambdaCDM's 36.5 at M = 2.8e+12 and 37.0 at the timing-argument 1.9e12, against an observed scatter of 34.1; the error on an rms from 18 points is about 5.7 km/s)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mutation controls and robustness
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  MUTATION 1 (nu = 1, i.e. Newtonian gravity with the same baryons): R0 falls from 1.09 to 0.55 Mpc, well outside the measurement's 0.15 Mpc error -- the kernel is load-bearing for the zero-velocity radius.  The rms in the outer window barely moves (36.0 to 36.9 km/s), which is the same lesson as check 99d: beyond 1.4 Mpc every model looks alike, and ALL of the information is in R0
  MUTATION 2 (permute which velocity belongs to which distance): rms about the framework's curve rises from 36.0 to 92.9 km/s -- the model curve is tracking the real distance-velocity relation and not just its mean
  ROBUSTNESS (widen to SBF and SN distances, Theta_1 < 0.5, out to 4 Mpc): N = 53, R0 = 0.76 Mpc against 0.88 -- the zero-velocity radius is not a property of the cut
  [PASS] 99e MUTATION CONTROLS behave: switching the kernel off collapses the predicted zero-velocity radius by far more than the measurement's own error; scrambling the distance-velocity pairing triples the residual scatter, so the model curve really is tracking the flow and not just its mean; and the measured R0 survives a substantial change of sample   (Newtonian R0 0.55 vs the framework's 1.09 Mpc against a measurement error of 0.15; permuted rms 92.9 vs 36.0 km/s; widened-sample R0 0.76 vs 0.88 Mpc)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT.  Item 99 asked for the Local Sheet's cold flow 'from baryons + Lambda with no dark mass'.  The
scatter itself turns out to be the wrong statistic -- it is 34 km/s and every model reproduces it,
because it is dominated by the real inhomogeneity of the Local Volume.  The statistic that bites is the
ZERO-VELOCITY RADIUS, and there the run produces something worth keeping AND something against interest.
Against interest: the framework's kernel applied naively to the Local Group's 2.2e+11 Msun of baryons
gives an effective braking mass of 1.2e+13 Msun at 1 Mpc and a zero-velocity radius of 2.72 Mpc,
3.1 times the measured 0.88 -- the MOND Local Group timing problem, reproduced here and not
explained away.  Worth keeping: the external field, taken unchanged from the 2M++ reconstruction in a
different script, saturates the boost beyond ~160 kpc and cuts that to 1.7e+12 Msun and R0 = 1.09 Mpc,
within 24 per cent of the measurement with no free parameter.  What it is NOT is a discriminant:
LambdaCDM gives R0 = 1.28 Mpc at M = 2.8e+12 and 1.12 at the timing-argument 1.9e12, and fits the same
galaxies as well within its own halo-mass uncertainty.  Recorded as
a CONSISTENCY, with the EFE identified as the load-bearing element and the naive-MOND failure recorded.
